control: The UI's role writes exactly the policy writes policy management makes, each with its history row
removes: the UI's role loses the delete on policy_rules, so lifting a rule is refused
packages: ./db/check
tests: TestTheUIWritesOnlyItsDecisionColumnsAndPolicyRules TestTheUIWritesOnlyItsDecisionColumnsAndPolicyRules/add,_edit_and_lift_an_account's_rule_with_its_history TestTheBasePolicyIsWrittenOnlyInABasePolicyTransaction
scheduled-count: no
integration: yes

diff --git a/db/migrations/00004_grants.sql b/db/migrations/00004_grants.sql
index eba1d5bc..67ddd013 100644
--- a/db/migrations/00004_grants.sql
+++ b/db/migrations/00004_grants.sql
@@ -492,7 +492,7 @@ DELETE ON oauth_clients TO mediated_mailbox_ui;
 -- An edit changes a rule's domain suffixes alone, since its scope and identifier never change. Its
 -- statements sit in db/policyrules/manage for an account's rules and db/policyrules/base for the base
 -- policy's, which only the UI's list admits. A confirmation of a candidate inserts its rule.
-GRANT INSERT, UPDATE (domain_suffix), DELETE ON policy_rules TO mediated_mailbox_ui;
+GRANT INSERT, UPDATE (domain_suffix) ON policy_rules TO mediated_mailbox_ui;
 -- Each policy write appends its history row, and the policy screens read the history. Insert alone,
 -- so evidence of who lifted a restriction survives the UI's compromise (ADR-0102).
 GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON policy_changes TO mediated_mailbox_ui;
